---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { email, duniaVideo } from "../../data.js";
---

<Base
  title="Dunia"
  description="Dunia is an SDK that captures gameplay at engine level and turns it into labelled training data for AI and robotics teams."
>
  <h1>Dunia</h1>
  <p>
    Dunia is an SDK. It sits inside a game engine, watches what happens while someone plays, and turns it
    into labelled training data for AI and robotics teams.
  </p>

  <div class="video">
    <iframe src={duniaVideo.embed} title="Dunia demo video" allow="autoplay; fullscreen" allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <p class="small">The MVP running in our demo game. <a href={duniaVideo.link}>Open it on Google Drive</a>.</p>

  <h2>Why games</h2>
  <p>
    A robot or a world model learns from three things: what the world looked like, what the agent did, and
    what happened next. Collecting that with real robots is slow and expensive, and sometimes you break the
    robot. There's very little of it on the web with the actions labelled.
  </p>
  <p>A game engine already tracks all three. Nobody has to label anything by hand.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The SDK hooks into the engine and records state, inputs and outcomes as the player acts.</li>
    <li>It saves a snapshot at each decision, not every frame.</li>
    <li>The export format is whatever the team using the data needs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it is</h2>
  <p>
    The MVP works, inside our own demo game. That's what the video shows. The next version gets built with
    the first teams who use it, so it fits a real problem instead of one we guessed at.
  </p>

  <h2>Talk to us</h2>
  <p>
    If you train robots or world models, buy training data, or want Dunia inside your own game,
    <a href={`mailto:${email}?subject=Dunia`}>email me</a>. I'll show you the demo in 15 minutes.
  </p>
</Base>
